title: "sc-hop: harden the data pool against rate-limit DoS vectors"

doc:
  - audience: Node Dev
    description: |
      Follow-up to #11662 closing several denial-of-service gaps in the HOP
      rate limiter and submission path:

      - Aggregate cross-account bandwidth bucket in `RateLimiter`. Per-account
        limits alone do not stop a coordinated set of authorized accounts from
        saturating the pool while each stays within its own allowance; one
        shared bucket now bounds total ingest.
      - The per-sender token-bucket map is bounded at
        `RateLimitConfig::max_tracked_senders`, so a flood of distinct sender
        accounts can no longer grow it without limit. When full, the sender
        with the most remaining token capacity is evicted — actively-limited
        senders stay tracked, so an exhausted attacker cannot re-enter with a
        fresh bucket by re-tripping the cap.
      - `MAX_DATA_SIZE` (2 MiB) now caps a single submission node-side,
        independently of the runtime-declared `max_promotion_size`; oversized
        payloads are rejected with `DataTooLarge` before touching the pool.
      - `HopDataPool::new` rejects a per-account or global `bandwidth_burst`
        smaller than the accounted cost of a max-size blob at full recipient
        fan-out. Such a bucket can never admit that entry however long the
        caller waits, so the `RateLimited { retry_after_secs }` hint would
        never come true. Surfaced as a new `HopError::InvalidConfig`
        (RPC error code 1023).
      - `HopError::DataTooLarge`'s second field widens from `u32` to `u64` so
        both the runtime `max_promotion_size` cap and the crate-level
        `MAX_DATA_SIZE` fit the same variant.

  - audience: Node Operator
    description: |
      New flags, all only relevant with `--enable-hop`:

      - `--hop-global-bandwidth-per-min-mib` — sustained aggregate
        cross-account bandwidth (default 1024)
      - `--hop-global-bandwidth-burst-mib` — aggregate bandwidth burst
        (default 2048)
      - `--hop-max-rate-limit-senders` — cap on senders tracked by the rate
        limiter, bounding its memory to roughly `N × 200` bytes
        (default 100 000)

      A node started with a `--hop-bandwidth-burst-mib` or
      `--hop-global-bandwidth-burst-mib` below the largest legal submission now
      fails fast at startup instead of silently rejecting those submissions
      forever.

crates:
  - name: sc-hop
    bump: major
